<h2 id="repetition">12.2 Repetition over dependency</h2>
<p>
  The instinct that produces drilling is a good one:
  <em>don't fetch the same thing twice</em>. For most of React's history, the only way to
  honor it was to fetch once at the top and share downward. Hooks and context were the
  first crack in that constraint; in Next, deduped fetches and cached functions remove it
  entirely.
  <strong
    >It is better for two components to ask the same question independently than to be
    wired together</strong
  >, because the framework guarantees the question is only answered once per request.
  <Chip k="next" />
  <Chip k="very" />
</p>

<Compare>
<CompareCol kind="bad" label="✕ Parent as data router">
    <pre
                class="code" is:raw
              ><span class="filename">app/programs/[id]/page.tsx</span><span class="tok-kw">export default async function</span> <span class="tok-fn">Page</span>({ params }) {
  <span class="tok-kw">const</span> { id } = <span class="tok-kw">await</span> params;
  <span class="tok-com">// This fetches the union of every child's needs.</span>
  <span class="tok-kw">const</span> program = <span class="tok-kw">await</span> <span class="tok-fn">getProgram</span>(id);
  <span class="tok-kw">return</span> (
    &lt;&gt;
      &lt;<span class="tok-fn">ProgramHeader</span>
        <span class="tok-prop">name</span>={program.name}
        <span class="tok-prop">status</span>={program.status}
        <span class="tok-prop">sponsor</span>={program.sponsor} /&gt;
      &lt;<span class="tok-fn">ProgramStats</span>
        <span class="tok-prop">enrolled</span>={program.enrolledCount}
        <span class="tok-prop">completed</span>={program.completedCount} /&gt;
    &lt;/&gt;
  );
}</pre>
<CodeCaption>
  The Header needs one more field? Edit Header, Page, and <code>getProgram</code>: three files, every time.
</CodeCaption>
  
</CompareCol>
<CompareCol kind="good" label="✓ Independent children, deduped data layer">
    <pre
                class="code" is:raw
              ><span class="filename">app/programs/[id]/page.tsx</span><span class="tok-kw">export default async function</span> <span class="tok-fn">Page</span>({ params }) {
  <span class="tok-kw">const</span> { id } = <span class="tok-kw">await</span> params;
  <span class="tok-com">// The only wire is identity.</span>
  <span class="tok-kw">return</span> (
    &lt;&gt;
      &lt;<span class="tok-fn">ProgramHeader</span> <span class="tok-prop">programId</span>={id} /&gt;
      &lt;<span class="tok-fn">ProgramStats</span>  <span class="tok-prop">programId</span>={id} /&gt;
    &lt;/&gt;
  );
}</pre>
    <pre
                class="code" is:raw
              ><span class="filename">components/program/ProgramHeader.tsx</span><span class="tok-kw">export async function</span> <span class="tok-fn">ProgramHeader</span>({ programId }) {
  <span class="tok-com">/* It asks for itself; the dedupe layer
     makes this free (§13). */</span>
  <span class="tok-kw">const</span> program = <span class="tok-kw">await</span> <span class="tok-fn">getProgram</span>(programId);
  <span class="tok-kw">return</span> &lt;header&gt;{program.name} …&lt;/header&gt;;
}</pre>
  
</CompareCol>
</Compare>
<p>
  Both <code>ProgramHeader</code> and <code>ProgramStats</code> call
  <code>getProgram(id)</code>; only one real request happens, because the data layer is
  deduped (<a href="13-dedupe-caching.html">§13</a> covers the mechanics:
  <code>cache()</code>, fetch dedupe, SWR keys). The page went from a data router back to
  what it should be: structure. And each child is now <strong>portable</strong>: drop it
  into any page, any parallel route, any dialog, hand it an id, and it works.
</p>

<Callout kind="info" title="This works on both sides of the boundary">
  <p>
    Server-side, independence is free via <code>React.cache()</code> and fetch dedupe.
    Client-side, the same pattern holds with <code>useSWR</code>/React Query: two
    components calling <code>useProgram(id)</code> share one cache entry and one request.
    What you should <em>not</em> do is hand-roll it: a fetch in an effect has no dedupe
    layer, so drilling becomes "necessary" again. The anti-patterns feed each other.
  </p>
</Callout>

<h2 id="streaming">12.3 Independence changes how the page streams</h2>
<p>
  Wiring isn't just a maintenance cost; it determines where your Suspense boundaries
  <em>can go</em>. A boundary must sit above whatever awaits. If the parent fetches and
  drills, the boundary is forced above the parent: the whole subtree is one all-or-nothing
  unit, gated by its slowest request. If children fetch for themselves, each child gets
  its own boundary inside the parent: the structure renders instantly and each region
  streams in as its answer arrives.
</p>

