<h2 id="callbacks">12.4 Callbacks up are wires too</h2>
<p>
  Passing data down is half the coupling story; the other half is children reporting back
  up. A callback prop that exists so a child can tell its parent "something changed,
  please refetch/re-render" is a wire pointing the other direction, and it usually means
  the coordination is happening at the wrong layer.
  <strong>Communicate through shared infrastructure instead of through parents</strong>:
  <Chip k="react" />
  <Chip k="critical" />
</p>
<ul>
  <li>
    <strong>The URL</strong>: filters, tabs, selection, pagination. A filter bar that
    calls <code>router.replace()</code> with new search params doesn't need to know a
    table exists; the table re-renders because the page's inputs changed (<a
      href="08-server-resolvable-state.html"
      >§8</a
    >).
  </li>
  <li>
    <strong>Cache invalidation</strong>: after a mutation,
    <code>revalidatePath</code>/<code>revalidateTag</code> on the server, or SWR/React
    Query invalidation on the client. Every component reading that data updates, with no
    parent orchestrating (<a href="13-dedupe-caching.html">§13</a>; the full mutation
    cycle is drawn in <a href="05-client-server-boundaries.html#refresh">§5.8</a>).
  </li>
</ul>
<p>
  Concretely: the callback you will be tempted to add most often is
  <code>onSuccess</code>: a mutation button reporting up so someone can refetch. Here is
  the same button, wired and wireless:
</p>

<Compare>
<CompareCol kind="bad" label="✕ Reports up through a wire">
    <pre
                class="code" is:raw
              ><span class="filename">PublishButton.tsx</span><span class="tok-dir">"use client"</span>;

<span class="tok-kw">function</span> <span class="tok-fn">PublishButton</span>({
  programId,
  onSuccess,
}: <span class="tok-fn">Props</span>) {
  <span class="tok-kw">return</span> (
    &lt;<span class="tok-fn">Button</span>
      <span class="tok-prop">onClick</span>={<span class="tok-kw">async</span> () =&gt; {
        <span class="tok-kw">await</span> <span class="tok-fn">publishProgram</span>(programId);
        <span class="tok-fn">onSuccess</span>();
      }}
    &gt;
      Publish
    &lt;/<span class="tok-fn">Button</span>&gt;
  );
}</pre>
<CodeCaption>
  The button must be handed a wire: someone above must exist, must care, and must never change shape.
</CodeCaption>
  
</CompareCol>
<CompareCol kind="good" label="✓ Invalidates the data's name">
    <pre
                class="code" is:raw
              ><span class="filename">PublishButton.tsx</span><span class="tok-dir">"use client"</span>;

<span class="tok-kw">function</span> <span class="tok-fn">PublishButton</span>({ programId }: <span class="tok-fn">Props</span>) {
  <span class="tok-kw">return</span> (
    &lt;<span class="tok-fn">Button</span>
      <span class="tok-prop">onClick</span>={() =&gt;
        <span class="tok-fn">startTransition</span>(<span class="tok-kw">async</span> () =&gt; {
          <span class="tok-com">/* The action revalidates its
             tag (§5.8); or, inside an
             SWR island, mutate the key: */</span>
          <span class="tok-kw">await</span> <span class="tok-fn">publishProgram</span>(programId);
          <span class="tok-kw">await</span> <span class="tok-fn">mutate</span>(
            [<span class="tok-str">"program"</span>, programId]
          );
        })
      }
    &gt;
      Publish
    &lt;/<span class="tok-fn">Button</span>&gt;
  );
}</pre>
<CodeCaption>
  No wire: the mutation invalidates the name the data lives under, and every reader refires on its own.
</CodeCaption>
  
</CompareCol>
</Compare>

<p>
  The <em>name</em> (a cache tag, a search param, an SWR key) is the entire contract
  between the leaf and its surroundings. This is deliberately the same
  <code>PublishButton</code> as
  <a href="05-client-server-boundaries.html#self-contained">§5.7</a>–<a
    href="05-client-server-boundaries.html#refresh"
    >§5.8</a
  >: that chapter argues the pattern as <em>boundary placement</em> (the leaf is the only
  client component, so the tree stays server), this one argues it as <em>wiring</em> (the
  leaf holds zero references to its surroundings, so the tree stays decoupled). Same code,
  two independent reasons it's right, and the two failures arrive together, too.
</p>
<p>
  Which is worth saying explicitly: the God component (<a
    href="05-client-server-boundaries.html#god-components"
    >§5.9</a
  >) is the maximal version of <em>this chapter's</em> failure as much as that one's. In
  wire terms, a <code>useProgramsManager()</code> that returns nine things has nine wires
  into every consumer; the tree stops being a tree of components and becomes a
  distribution network for one object's shape. Every feature threads new props through
  components that don't use them, every signature change ripples to every consumer. And
  because that same hook says <code>"use client"</code> at the top, the wiring cost and
  the boundary cost compound: the structure that couples everything is the structure that
  client-renders everything.
</p>
<p>
  Legitimate exceptions: genuinely local, ephemeral coordination: a form field telling its
  form it changed, a listbox item telling its listbox it's highlighted. The test is
  whether the two components form <em>one</em> composite widget with one responsibility,
  or whether a callback is smuggling app-level data flow through the component tree.
</p>

<h2 id="rules">The rules</h2>
<table class="tbl">
  <thead>
    <tr>
      <th>Rule</th>
      <th>Scope</th>
      <th>Importance</th>
    </tr>
  </thead>
  <tbody>
    <tr>
      <td>
        <strong>Never pass a child anything the parent doesn't itself need.</strong>
        Identity, slots, and parent-owned config are the exceptions.
      </td>
      <td><Chip k="react" /></td>
      <td><Chip k="critical" /></td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
      <td>
        Don't pass external data to children in parallel:
        <strong>let each child ask the deduped data layer itself</strong>.
      </td>
      <td><Chip k="next" /></td>
      <td><Chip k="very" /></td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
      <td>Prefer <strong>repetition backed by dedupe</strong> over shared plumbing.</td>
      <td><Chip k="next" /></td>
      <td><Chip k="very" /></td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
      <td>
        App-level communication goes <strong>through the URL or the cache</strong>, not
        callbacks up the tree.
      </td>
      <td><Chip k="react" /></td>
      <td><Chip k="critical" /></td>
    </tr>
  </tbody>
</table>

